 ## Overview
-Decoupled SVO Compression is a memory optimization technique for Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVOs) that separates the geometric topology from voxel attributes (such as color, normals, or reflectance) to allow independent, specialized compression of each component [S1]. Unlike standard SVOs or naive colored DAGs that store attributes redundantly with every node, this method achieves significant memory reduction by compressing the sparse geometry structure and applying lossless palette-based compression to the decoupled attribute array, outperforming existing methods like PSVOs, ESVOs, and CDAGs in both memory footprint and visual quality [S1].
+Decoupled SVO Compression is a memory optimization technique for Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVOs) that separates geometric topology from voxel attributes (such as color, normals, or reflectance) to allow independent, specialized compression of each component [S1]. Unlike standard SVOs or naive colored DAGs that store attributes redundantly with every node, this method achieves significant memory reduction by compressing the sparse geometry structure and applying lossless palette-based compression to the decoupled attribute data, outperforming existing methods like PSVOs, ESVOs, and CDAGs in both memory footprint and visual quality [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Input:** A Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) or Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) representing a voxel scene, where each leaf or internal node may possess associated attributes (e.g., RGB color, surface normals) [S1].
-*   **Output:** A compressed data structure consisting of a compacted geometry array and a separate, compressed attribute array, along with metadata required for traversal and index reconstruction [S1].
